            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>How to Allocate Budget Most Reasonably During Cold Start?</h2>
<p>During the cold start phase of an independent website, it's recommended to allocate 60%-70% of the budget to testing.</p>
<p>Specifically, the first two weeks should focus on small amounts across multiple platforms, with a daily budget of $20-50 per ad group, prioritizing 2-3 main platforms (usually Meta and Google). The remaining 30% of the budget is reserved for conversion optimization—gradually increase investment once you identify creatives with click-through rates exceeding 1%. A home goods independent website using this approach achieved a stable ROI above 1.8 during the testing phase before scaling.</p>
<h2>What's the Right Budget Ratio for Meta, Google, and TikTok?</h2>
<p>The reference ratio for mature independent websites is Meta 40%, Google 30%, TikTok 30%.</p>
<p>But this isn't a fixed formula—it should be adjusted based on your product category. For standard products with high-intent users (like 3C accessories), you can increase Google's share to over 40%, as search traffic has a shorter conversion path. For non-standard products targeting younger demographics (like trendy clothing), you can raise TikTok's budget to 40%. If a platform hasn't generated data after 2-3 weeks, it's recommended to cut its budget to below 20% and concentrate resources on platforms showing positive data.</p>
<h2>How Much Budget Is Needed for Testing vs. Scaling Phases?</h2>
<p>Testing phase: For a single category, it's recommended to prepare $3,000-$5,000 to complete a round of creative testing.</p>
<p>Scaling phase: Once you've confirmed 1-2 sets of creatives with ROI exceeding 1.5, start increasing budget in weekly increments—30% in the first week, 50% in the second week, then observe whether data remains stable before continuing. A beauty independent website's experience shows that scaling daily budget from $200 to $800 took three weeks, and this rhythm was relatively healthy, avoiding account fluctuations caused by sudden traffic changes.</p>
<h2>How to Determine Budget Caps for Different Categories?</h2>
<p>The budget cap depends on your average order value and profit margin.</p>
<p>The formula is: Daily Budget ≤ Daily Order Target × Average Order Value × Minimum Acceptable ROI. Assuming an average order value of $80, target of 50 daily orders, and minimum acceptable ROI of 1.5, the daily budget cap is $2,666. If your profit margin is only 30%, the acceptable ROI must be at least 1.5 to break even, which locks your budget space. In this case, you can only break through by optimizing creatives and landing pages to improve conversion rates.</p>
<h2>What Are the Signals for Budget Adjustments?</h2>
<p>Three key signals:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>CPM continues to rise by more than 15%</strong>: This indicates audience fatigue and requires new creatives or expanded audience packages</li>
<li><strong>Cost per order exceeds 40% of average order value</strong>: For example, if the average order value is $100, a CPA exceeding $40 leaves basically no profit margin—immediately pause or significantly reduce spending</li>
<li><strong>ROAS drops below break-even line twice within three days</strong>: Don't wait for weekly data—immediately stop loss-making ad groups and shift to testing new creatives</li>
</ol>
<h2>How to Manage Unified Budget Control with Multiple Accounts?</h2>
<p>For multi-account teams, it's recommended to implement a dual-track mechanism combining balance alerts and daily spend monitoring.</p>
<p>Set daily spending caps for each account, such as $500 per account per day, with automatic pausing when exceeded. At the same time, Yiyu Digital Media's multi-account management dashboard allows unified viewing of all account balances and daily spending, triggering alerts when balances fall below 3 days' worth of consumption—this prevents account depletion from affecting data accumulation. A 3C accessories team using this mechanism reduced losses due to insufficient balance by 60%.</p>
<p><strong>In one sentence</strong>: Budget allocation isn't fixed from the start—it needs dynamic adjustment following the rhythm of testing, validation, and scaling. The core principle is to concentrate 80% of resources on high-ROI creatives and platforms.</p>
